    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d60">

                <pb facs="0195" n="173" xml:id="pg173"/>

                <head>60. National Security Action Memorandum No. 158<anchor n="0" xml:id="d60-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1962-05-31">May 31,
                    1962</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <list type="to">
                    <head>TO</head>
                    <item>Secretary of State</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Contingency Plans for Berlin</item>
                </list>
                <p>The President has now approved the development of contingency plans for Berlin on
                    the basis of acceptance of a requirement for passports for West German access to
                    West Berlin, but rejection of any attempt by East German authorities to require
                    visas for such access.</p>
                <p>In approving such contingency planning, the President explicitly reserves the
                    right of the United States Government to review its position in the light of the
                    actual development of events. In particular, approval of this planning should
                    imply no automatic American undertaking to extend military protection to
                    civilian traffic under any specific set of circumstances.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">McGeorge
                                Bundy</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
